WEBVTT

00:00:02.250 --> 00:00:04.050
Will: Hi, I'm Will from Bolt.

00:00:04.050 --> 00:00:06.090
In this video, you'll
learn how Bolt fits

00:00:06.090 --> 00:00:09.840
into Microsoft Workflows to help
you turn ideas into governed,

00:00:09.840 --> 00:00:11.790
production-ready applications.

00:00:11.790 --> 00:00:15.360
Then, my colleague, Joe, and I
will demo three key features

00:00:15.360 --> 00:00:16.920
which enable this process.

00:00:16.920 --> 00:00:19.860
Prompt-driven development has
already changed what individual

00:00:19.860 --> 00:00:20.640
creators can do.

00:00:20.640 --> 00:00:24.360
A product manager, designer, or
developer can describe an idea

00:00:24.360 --> 00:00:27.330
and quickly get a working
prototype, internal tool,

00:00:27.330 --> 00:00:29.070
or new feature on screen.

00:00:29.070 --> 00:00:31.950
That speed is powerful because
it shortens the distance

00:00:31.950 --> 00:00:35.160
between an idea and something
people can actually use.

00:00:35.160 --> 00:00:39.450
But for enterprise teams, speed
on its own is not enough,

00:00:39.450 --> 00:00:43.170
especially for the developers
and IT teams responsible

00:00:43.170 --> 00:00:46.320
for what happens after
something is generated.

00:00:46.320 --> 00:00:49.440
Most AI app building tools
assume you are starting

00:00:49.440 --> 00:00:51.090
from a blank canvas.

00:00:51.090 --> 00:00:53.790
That is useful for prototypes
and some internal tools,

00:00:53.790 --> 00:00:56.880
but it's not how most
enterprise software works.

00:00:56.880 --> 00:01:00.360
Large organizations already
have existing applications,

00:01:00.360 --> 00:01:03.360
governed cloud environments,
developer workflows,

00:01:03.360 --> 00:01:06.300
design standards, and
security requirements.

00:01:06.300 --> 00:01:10.110
So the developer problem is not
just, can AI generate an app?

00:01:10.110 --> 00:01:13.130
It is, can this fit into
what we already have?

00:01:13.130 --> 00:01:15.920
Contains increment
existing applications,

00:01:15.920 --> 00:01:18.620
add features safely, and
give developers code

00:01:18.620 --> 00:01:20.390
that they can review, govern,

00:01:20.390 --> 00:01:23.840
and deploy without rewriting
everything from scratch.

00:01:23.840 --> 00:01:26.960
The real question for a
Microsoft build audience is,

00:01:26.960 --> 00:01:30.800
how do you make AI-powered app
creation faster while keeping

00:01:30.800 --> 00:01:34.680
developers in control of the
code, approved foundations,

00:01:34.680 --> 00:01:37.350
environments, and
deployment path?

00:01:37.350 --> 00:01:39.750
That is exactly what
Bolt's collaboration

00:01:39.750 --> 00:01:42.330
with Microsoft is
designed to support.

00:01:42.330 --> 00:01:46.350
With Bolt on Microsoft
Azure and Microsoft 365,

00:01:46.350 --> 00:01:48.360
teams start from
the conversation

00:01:48.360 --> 00:01:51.870
and context they already have,
then move towards applications

00:01:51.870 --> 00:01:53.820
that fit inside the
infrastructure they

00:01:53.820 --> 00:01:55.170
already trust.

00:01:55.170 --> 00:01:58.830
Bolt-generated front-end code
can be reviewed, extended,

00:01:58.830 --> 00:02:00.480
and deployed into Azure.

00:02:00.480 --> 00:02:02.880
Teams can connect that
work into their GitHub

00:02:02.880 --> 00:02:06.210
and Azure DevOps workflows,
Microsoft identity

00:02:06.210 --> 00:02:09.270
and security tooling and the
governance patterns enterprise

00:02:09.270 --> 00:02:11.100
developers already use.

00:02:11.100 --> 00:02:14.160
That means developers don't have
to be the first person prompting

00:02:14.160 --> 00:02:15.720
in Bolt to benefit from it.

00:02:15.720 --> 00:02:18.450
They can define the standards,
approve foundations,

00:02:18.450 --> 00:02:20.640
environments, and
deployment path

00:02:20.640 --> 00:02:24.300
so creators can move faster
while the output still fits the

00:02:24.300 --> 00:02:26.940
organization's
technical foundation.

00:02:26.940 --> 00:02:29.820
So Bolt is not just a faster
way to generate an app,

00:02:29.820 --> 00:02:33.540
but it's a controlled path
from idea to application.

00:02:33.540 --> 00:02:37.380
The three pieces we'll cover in
the demo are design systems.

00:02:37.380 --> 00:02:39.750
This gives every team
a shared foundation.

00:02:39.750 --> 00:02:43.350
An organization can make its
approved design system available

00:02:43.350 --> 00:02:46.890
in bulk so every project starts
with the right components,

00:02:46.890 --> 00:02:49.980
patterns, and brand standards
instead of a blank canvas.

00:02:49.980 --> 00:02:52.920
The second is the Bolt
command line interface.

00:02:52.920 --> 00:02:55.200
This gives developers
a programmatic way

00:02:55.200 --> 00:02:56.550
to interact with Bolt.

00:02:56.550 --> 00:03:00.270
They can bring in approved
assets, connect existing repos

00:03:00.270 --> 00:03:03.060
and workflows, and make the
organization's technical

00:03:03.060 --> 00:03:05.370
standards available
to the broader team.

00:03:05.370 --> 00:03:07.320
Finally is the Bolt interface.

00:03:07.320 --> 00:03:10.380
This gives everyone on
the team an intuitive,

00:03:10.380 --> 00:03:12.000
capable place to build.

00:03:12.000 --> 00:03:14.160
Product managers,
designers, operators,

00:03:14.160 --> 00:03:16.350
and developers can
prompt, iterate,

00:03:16.350 --> 00:03:19.530
and create features while still
working within the foundations

00:03:19.530 --> 00:03:21.180
that developers have approved.

00:03:21.180 --> 00:03:22.410
The goal is simple.

00:03:22.410 --> 00:03:25.740
Accelerate app creation for
the whole organization while

00:03:25.740 --> 00:03:29.190
preserving governance, security,
and developer standards.

